### Preserve nonzero duration for one-step transitions

**Severity:** `medium`

When `firstStepWasImmediate` removes the only step, `OriginalDuration` returns zero and the animation is skipped even though there is still a visible zoom delta. Keep the invalid/no-op cases at zero, but clamp real one-step transitions to at least one configured step so they animate instead of jumping.

```suggestion
std::uint64_t ZoomAnimation::Duration(float currentZoom, float targetZoom, bool firstStepWasImmediate, std::uint64_t stepTimeMilliseconds)
{
    if (currentZoom <= 0.0f || targetZoom <= 0.0f || currentZoom == targetZoom)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    constexpr std::uint64_t durationNumerator = 3;
    constexpr std::uint64_t durationDenominator = 2;
    const auto originalDuration = OriginalDuration(currentZoom, targetZoom, firstStepWasImmediate, stepTimeMilliseconds);
    return std::max(originalDuration, stepTimeMilliseconds) * durationNumerator / durationDenominator;
}
```

### Do not call magnifier smoothing after failed live-window creation

**Severity:** `high`

This smoothing call runs even if the live zoom window was not created, so `g_hWndLiveZoomMag` can still be null or unavailable on the non-DWM/fullscreen path. The show handler already restores smoothing after the control is known to exist; remove the extra call and continue directly to the recording-owner update.

```suggestion
                if ( g_RecordToggle )
                {
                    g_SelectRectangle.UpdateOwner( g_hWndLiveZoom );
                }
```

### Keep the hidden live zoom window cache bounded

**Severity:** `medium`

The current code keeps the hidden live zoom magnifier resident indefinitely on current Windows, so its fullscreen surfaces remain cached for the rest of the process after a single activation. Keep the warm-cache optimization, but arm the existing timeout for all supported versions so the resources are released after the idle period.

```suggestion
            // Keep the magnifier control warm for a bounded idle period. Re-creating it costs ~200ms while warm and
            // can take much longer after its fullscreen surfaces have left the standby list.
            KillTimer( hWnd, 1 );
            SetTimer( hWnd, 1, LIVEZOOM_WINDOW_TIMEOUT, NULL );
```

### Drop back to the live-refresh timer after animation completes

**Severity:** `medium`

Once a live zoom animation settles, the timer remains at the 10 ms animation cadence even though only normal live refreshes are needed. Reset the timer to the live-refresh cadence at the same point smoothing is restored so idle live zoom does not keep repainting unnecessarily fast.

```suggestion
                if( !zoomAnimation.IsActive() ) {

                    if( animationSmoothingForced ) {

                        animationSmoothingForced = FALSE;
                        if( pMagSetLensUseBitmapSmoothing )
                            pMagSetLensUseBitmapSmoothing( g_hWndLiveZoomMag, g_SmoothImage );
                    }
                    SetTimer( hWnd, 0, LIVEZOOM_REFRESH_FRAME_TIME, NULL );
                }
```

### Keep the fractional viewport when entering annotation modes

**Severity:** `medium`

The fractional source viewport is used while zooming, but the current code falls back to integer coordinates as soon as drawing, tracing, or typing starts. That can visibly shift the magnified content when the user begins annotating; keep the same fractional viewport for annotation modes so the view remains stable.

```suggestion
            // Keep the same fractional viewport while drawing and typing so entering annotation cannot shift the
            // magnified content by up to one source pixel.
            float sourceX;
            float sourceY;
            GetAnimatedZoomSourceCoordinates( zoomLevel, &cursorPos, width, height, &sourceX, &sourceY );

            const int sourceLeft = static_cast<int>( std::floor( sourceX ) );
            const int sourceTop = static_cast<int>( std::floor( sourceY ) );
            const int sourceRight = min( width, static_cast<int>( std::ceil( sourceX + static_cast<float>( width ) / zoomLevel ) ) );
            const int sourceBottom = min( height, static_cast<int>( std::ceil( sourceY + static_cast<float>( height ) / zoomLevel ) ) );
            const int srcW = sourceRight - sourceLeft;
            const int srcH = sourceBottom - sourceTop;
            // Enlarge with StretchBlt (so HALFTONE interpolates) and offset the destination to compensate the
            // sub-pixel origin, giving smooth device-pixel-granular motion instead of source-pixel snapping.
            const int destX = static_cast<int>( std::lround( ( static_cast<float>( sourceLeft ) - sourceX ) * zoomLevel ) );
            const int destY = static_cast<int>( std::lround( ( static_cast<float>( sourceTop ) - sourceY ) * zoomLevel ) );
            const int destW = static_cast<int>( std::lround( static_cast<float>( srcW ) * zoomLevel ) );
            const int destH = static_cast<int>( std::lround( static_cast<float>( srcH ) * zoomLevel ) );
            StretchBlt( ps.hdc,
                    destX, destY, destW, destH,
                    hdcScreenCompat,
                    sourceLeft, sourceTop, srcW, srcH,
                    SRCCOPY|CAPTUREBLT );
```
